[{:seon.task/name "a-keys-levels-are-its-schemas"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner ruling 2026-10-29 (docs/decisions.md, config at every level, no env var switches): a setting can be set at every level, in this order, later wins: shipped defaults < profile (--profile) < --config FILE < --set < main's root entity < model < provider < task < agent; and each key's schema names the levels it may be set at. What exists: `:seon.task/config` is a closed map of six config keys, a second list beside the schemas; ``:seon.config/place`` marks per-process keys; nothing says which keys may be set at runtime, per model, per task or per agent. Change: schema entries carry ``:seon.config/levels``, a new key, a subset of #{:boot :runtime :model :provider :task :agent} (default #{:boot}: shipped, profile, --config, --set); `:seon.task/config`'s and the agent's keys are derived from the keys whose levels have :task / :agent (the six get :task); `seon.config/resolve-in` and task declaration refuse a key set at a level its schema does not name, with an error naming key, level and the levels it has; the config panel shows each key's levels; docs/config.md gets a table of keys by level generated by the same read. Replaces the hand list in seon.task.edn. Proof: a task declaring a :boot-only key is refused; a :task key resolves with source [:task]; every key in the six-key list has :task."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/a-key-set-at-a-level-its-schema-excludes-is-refused seon.config-test/every-task-config-key-has-the-task-level}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-resolver-reads-main-s-copy"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `cfg/value` (121 call sites) reads the boot map; only cfg/provider-at merges `seon.config/from-db` main, so a change to main's root entity applies to the provider alone, though the ruling puts main's root entity above --set and below the model. Change: `seon.config/resolve-in` takes the config in force as `(merge config (from-db main))` keyed by main's commit (a reference, not a held db: AGENTS 4), so the runtime layer applies to every key whose levels have :runtime, with source [:main]; `seon.config/sources` names it; the three `(get cfg/config k)` bypasses (root.clj; fork_server.clj; my/root.clj) go through `cfg/value`. Measure: calls per turn and bytes of a resolve before/after (a cost proof, work not time). Deletes provider-at's own merge."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.config/a-keys-levels-are-its-schemas"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/a-runtime-change-on-main-applies-to-the-next-read seon.config-test/provider-at-is-the-resolver}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-can-carry-its-own-config"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner ruling 2026-10-29: the agent is a level beside the task, above it (config 'added to an agent's entity to override on a per agent level'). What exists: an agent is a forked process running one task; its identity for the resolver is the task id, and no level is its own. Change: ctx gains `:agent`; `seon.config/resolve-in` gets one clause above the task's, reading the declared :seon.config.* attributes of the agent's run entity whose key names :agent in its levels (the same flattening task.clj does for a task); `(my.root/run! sys [id] {:config {...}})` writes them; `seon.config/sources` reports [:agent]. Reuses the one read; adds one clause and no attribute type. Proof: two runs of one task with different :seon.config.ai/model each use theirs, and a key without :agent in its levels is refused there."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.config/the-resolver-reads-main-s-copy"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/an-agent-s-setting-wins-over-its-task-s seon.root-test/two-runs-of-a-task-use-their-own-model}}
 {:seon.task/name "per-run-keys-ride-the-command-line"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner ruling 2026-10-29: bin/ passes --set, not env vars. What exists: bin/test, bin/check, bin/root, bin/env and test_store.clj set SEON_WIKI_BASELINE, RUNS, STORE, MAIN, SOURCE_ROOT, RESULTS_DIR, COST_MAX_N, SEEDS, EMBED_TEXTS, WEB_PORT, SERVER_PORT, INSTRUMENT, AI_MODEL, RUN_CHECKOUT, which default.edn reads with `#env` under --set (the stale-local.edn failure docs/config.md already names); fx.clj strips the leaked ones from test children; config/local.edn holds two `#env`. Change: delete the remaining `#env` tags and local.edn's two (bin/ports writes plain port values); bin/ scripts and test_store pass `--set`, as they already pass `--profile`; one function, to be written (``seon.config/args``), builds a child's `--set` list from the keys marked ``:seon.config/place`` (read in seon.fx, not a registered schema key); the strip lists keep only launcher variables; the no-env-tag test's exception set (SEON_AI_MODEL, SEON_INSTRUMENT) empties and the test becomes `default.edn reads no environment`. Measure: verdict reach digests move once (state it), `bin/check config` green. Deletes: 14 env exports, the strip entries, config.md's warning."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/default-edn-reads-no-environment seon.config-test/a-child-gets-its-keys-as-set-arguments seon.config-test/bin-ports-writes-plain-ports}}
 {:seon.task/name "config-page-lists-levels-env-and-process-knobs"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: docs/config.md has layers, the profile and the resolver but no table of levels, of the environment variables that stay (SEON_CHECKOUT, the API-key variable, launcher JOLT_*/HOME), or of process knobs (machine.md has the heap ones). Change, finished here after the tasks above: one 'Levels' table (the ruling's order, each level's source name), one 'Environment' table (each remaining variable, who reads it, why), one 'Process settings' table (JOLT_MAX_HEAP/NEW_SIZE, MAX_HEAP_FREE_RATIO, sweepers, COMPILER_OPTIONS, native libs: key, edge function, process, when), linked from machine.md, model.md (SEON_AI_MODEL becomes --set) and the default.edn header. bin/root drops its repeated JOLT_*_LIB line by sourcing bin/env with the tree's ROOT. Proof: a test reads each table entry's key and edge function and checks it exists."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.config/per-run-keys-ride-the-command-line" "seon.config/an-agent-can-carry-its-own-config"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/every-process-knob-names-its-key-and-edge-function}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-config-in-force-is-its-own-entity"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner's stage 3 (docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09: the config in force an entity of its own, linked from the system entity), asked early by Web, 2026-11-09: root's entity holds root's attributes and 174 seon.config.* keys, so by best match it is the config (10 reds in Web's 2a spike). Plan (Optimization, agreed with Web): an entity whose identity is the content id of the config map in force (seon.lib/id), holding the seon.config.* attributes, so the same config is one entity on every branch; root's entity gets one ref, ``:seon.root/config`` (stage 3 renames it with the system entity); seon.config/facts and the boot write in start! write the entity and the ref; seon.config/from-db and every reader of config keys on root's entity follow the ref; the config keys leave root's entity schema. Store erase (config attributes move entities), Web and Context warned. About +30 -20. Lands in the erase window. The held branch jolt/config-dissolve (each config key its own schema) is separate and still waits for Sean's choice of how a key declares it is stored. Landing order (Optimization, agreed with Web, 2026-11-09; docs/plan.md's Now): (0) FIRST, jolt/index-arg with its Jolt :private? pin (``seon.source/analysis-never-writes-the-image``), as soon as the pin is proved: it is independent of the erase window, and 2a, 2b and stage 4 wait on it; (1) the erase window: the config in force as its own entity (``seon.config/the-config-in-force-is-its-own-entity``), Context's message-delivery re-model (no ``:seon.message/call``), a cite is a ref (``seon.ref/a-cite-is-a-ref-to-what-exists``), with one store erase, Web and Context warned first; (2) jolt/sync (stage 5) with jolt/dbboot (1d) merged into it, landed as one, its up-merge reviewed before 1d goes in; (3) jolt/hiccup with stage 4, its consumer, after Web's browser check of the placeholders. Every landing message goes to the Web session (running point) before bin/land."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
]
